<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lippe (river)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lippe is a river in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, about 220 km long, rising near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paderborn">Paderborn</a> and flowing west through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a> to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wesel">Wesel</a>. In antiquity it was a main route of Roman advances into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania">Germania</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionary</a> bases such as Oberaden and Haltern along its banks. It gave its name to the County of Lippe.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lippe-river.jpg" alt="Lippe (river)" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wolfgang Hunscher, Dortmund · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lippe_in_Luenen_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
